  "worked_solution": "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}⁡X=\\frac{{log}_{b}⁡X}{{log}_{b}⁡a}\n$$\n\nWe want to change from base $a=1000$ to base $b=10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{1000}⁡x & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡x}{{log}_{10}⁡(1000)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the expression in the denominator using exponents, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡x}{{log}_{10}⁡(1000)} & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡x}{{log}_{10}⁡({10}^{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡x}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\operatorname{log}⁡x=t,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n{log}_{1000}⁡x=\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡x}{3}=\\frac{t}{3}.\n$$",
